  "id": "Compound::DB01142 - Gene::1576",
  "verbalization": "Doxepin is implicated in the regulation of Cyp3a4 through a series of indirect pathways involving the nervous system and organic compounds. The evidence suggests that Doxepin, through its effects on chronic insomnia or numbness of the tongue, leads to the activation of Cyp3a4 via binding to specific agents that affect the nervous system or are organic heteropolycyclic compounds. These agents, in turn, interact with Cyp3a4, indicating a potential role in the metabolic processing of substrates. The shared lowest common ancestors between Doxepin and the agents involved—such as the nervous system or organic compounds—suggests a broader mechanism of action that may be conserved across different pathways. While the exact nature of the interaction remains unclear, the integrated evidence points to a possible dependency on Cyp3a4 in the metabolic regulation of these effects, highlighting the importance of Cyp3a4 in the pharmacological response to Doxepin.",
